    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to consider the ways to arrange the math books, the English books, and the two groups of books (math and English).\n\n1. **Treat the groups of books as single entities**: Since the math books must stay together and the English books must stay together, we can think of these two groups as two separate items.\n   - The math books can be treated as one item.\n   - The English books can be treated as another item.\n\n2. **Arrange the two groups**: There are 2 ways to arrange these two groups on the shelf. These are:\n   - Math group first, English group second\n   - English group first, Math group second\n\n3. **Arrange the books within each group**: \n   - The 3 math books can be arranged among themselves in \\(3!\\) (3 factorial) ways.\n     \\[\n     3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n     \\]\n   - The 5 English books can be arranged among themselves in \\(5!\\) (5 factorial) ways.\n     \\[\n     5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n     \\]\n\n4. **Combine the arrangements**: To find the total number of ways to arrange the books, we multiply the number of ways to arrange the groups by the number of ways to arrange the books within each group.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total arrangements} = (\\text{Number of ways to arrange the groups}) \\times (\\text{Number of ways to arrange the math books}) \\times (\\text{Number of ways to arrange the English books})\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total arrangements} = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the final product**:\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 6 = 12\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   12 \\times 120 = 1440\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the books is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
